In traditional web applications, authorization is usually a perimeter gate: verify the user's JWT when the HTTP request arrives, check their role, and let the backend controller run.

In an autonomous AI system, a single perimeter check at login is dangerously insufficient.

Between the moment a user sends a prompt and the moment a durable worker opens a network socket to an enterprise API, the system searches vector indexes, ranks thousands of tools, drafts multi-step plans, and potentially waits hours for human approval. If permissions are only checked at turn ingress—or if the AI runtime holds an ambient service token—the agent becomes a **Confused Deputy** with more authority than the human who invoked it.

{% callout tone="insight" title="The Law of Delegated AI Authority" %}
An AI agent must never possess intrinsic permissions of its own. Its effective authority at any millisecond is the strict intersection of the **authenticated human principal**, the **assistant profile ceiling**, the **live resource ownership check**, and a **single-attempt cryptographic grant**.
{% /callout %}

---

